### Gotcha 1: split() takes a regex, not a literal

The most common trap is that `split()` interprets its argument as a regular expression, not a plain string. Regex special characters like `.`, `|`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `(`, `)`, `[`, `{`, `\`, `^`, and `$` have special meaning. A dot matches any single character, so splitting on `"."` matches every character position and returns an empty array.

```java
"192.168.1.1".split(".");            // [] (empty! "." matches any char)
"192.168.1.1".split("\\.");          // ["192", "168", "1", "1"] (escaped)
"192.168.1.1".split(Pattern.quote(".")); // ["192", "168", "1", "1"] (safe)
"192.168.1.1".split("[.]");          // ["192", "168", "1", "1"] (character class)
```

`Pattern.quote()` is the safest approach because it escapes any special characters automatically. Use it when the delimiter comes from user input or configuration.

### Gotcha 2: trailing empty strings are removed by default

By default, `split()` removes trailing empty strings from the result array. This means if your data has a meaningful empty trailing field, it silently disappears.

```java
"a,,b,".split(",");      // ["a", "", "b"]    – trailing empty REMOVED!
"a,,b,".split(",", -1);  // ["a", "", "b", ""] – trailing empty PRESERVED
```

In banking CSV processing, a missing trailing field is meaningful. If a transaction record has the format `txnId,fromAccount,toAccount,amount,note` and the note is empty, the default split drops it. Always use `split(delimiter, -1)` for data parsing.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TransactionParser {
    // BAD: loses trailing empty fields
    public String[] parseBad(String line) {
        return line.split(",");  // trailing empties removed!
    }

    // GOOD: preserves all fields
    public String[] parseGood(String line) {
        return line.split(",", -1);  // trailing empties preserved
    }
}

// Example:
String record = "TXN001,SAV2001,CUR3001,50000,";
parseBad(record).length;   // 4 (note field is lost!)
parseGood(record).length;  // 5 (note field is empty string)
```

### Gotcha 3: splitting an empty string

Splitting an empty string returns an array with one empty element, not an empty array. This trips up length checks.

```java
"".split(",");        // [""] (array with one empty element, NOT empty array)
"".split(",").length; // 1 (not 0!)

// To handle this, check the content not just the array
String[] parts = input.split(",", -1);
if (parts.length == 1 && parts[0].isEmpty()) {
    // input was empty – handle accordingly
}
```

### When to use a proper CSV library

`split()` cannot handle quoted fields, which are standard in CSV. A field value containing the delimiter breaks the parse.

```java
// BAD: split does not handle quoted fields
String line = "Rajesh,\"National Bank, Mumbai\",500000";
line.split(",");  // ["Rajesh", "\"National Bank", " Mumbai\"", "500000"] (broken!)

// GOOD: use OpenCSV or Apache Commons CSV
import com.opencsv.CSVReader;
import com.opencsv.CSVReaderBuilder;
import java.io.StringReader;

CSVReader reader = new CSVReaderBuilder(new StringReader(line)).build();
String[] fields = reader.readNext();  // ["Rajesh", "National Bank, Mumbai", "500000"]
```

In banking applications at National Bank of India, Rajesh uses Apache Commons CSV for all file parsing because transaction descriptions often contain commas, quotes, and newlines.

### What about the limit parameter?

`split(regex, limit)` controls how many parts to produce:

| Limit value | Behavior |
|---|---|
| 0 (default) | Remove trailing empties, no limit on count |
| -1 | Keep trailing empties, no limit on count |
| n (positive) | At most n parts; last part contains the remainder |

```java
"a,b,c,d".split(",", 2);   // ["a", "b,c,d"] – split into exactly 2 parts
"a,b,c,d".split(",", -1);  // ["a", "b", "c", "d"] – all parts preserved
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Pattern.compile() and reuse** for repeated splits: `Pattern.compile(",").split(line)` avoids recompiling the regex each time in a hot loop.
- **In Java 8+, use streams** for complex tokenization: `Pattern.compile(",").splitAsStream(line).map(String::trim).toList()`.
- **For simple single-character delimiters,** some libraries provide non-regex split methods that are faster. Guava's `Splitter.on(',').splitToList(line)` is explicit and avoids regex pitfalls.
